// Google Calendar OAuth credentials for MeetingBarNG.
//
// This file supplies the OAuth client the app signs in with. Copy it to
// `GoogleSecrets.xcconfig` (same folder). That copy is git-ignored;
// `Project.xcconfig` includes it automatically when present.
//
// YOU MAY NOT NEED THIS. Users can supply their own OAuth client at runtime in
// Preferences > Calendars > "Use my own Google credentials", which works in a
// build that carries no credentials at all. Fill this in when you want Google
// Calendar to work out of the box for whoever runs your build.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Creating the client
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   1. Google Cloud Console > https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
//      Create or select a project, then enable the "Google Calendar API".
//
//   2. Configure the OAuth consent screen (User type: External). While the app
//      is unverified, add your own Google account under "Test users" or you will
//      not be able to sign in.
//
//   3. Create an "OAuth client ID" of type **Desktop app**.
//
//      Desktop app is the required type, NOT iOS. The app receives the OAuth
//      redirect on a loopback HTTP listener (http://127.0.0.1:<random port>),
//      which Google documents as the recommended redirect for macOS desktop
//      apps and supports only for the Desktop app client type. The reversed
//      domain custom scheme an iOS client uses will not work here: a custom
//      scheme must be declared in the app's Info.plist at BUILD time, so it can
//      never receive the redirect for a client supplied at runtime, and any
//      other app on the Mac can register the same scheme - which is why Google
//      is retiring custom schemes over app impersonation.
//
//      No redirect URI needs to be registered in the console. Loopback ports are
//      matched dynamically.
//
//   4. GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID accepts either form the console shows you: the full id
//      (1234567890-abc123.apps.googleusercontent.com) or just the part before
//      `.apps.googleusercontent.com`. Paste whichever you have.
//
//   5. GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET: a Desktop app client has one - paste it. If your
//      client has none, leave it EMPTY (not the placeholder text).
//
//      Note that this "secret" is not one in any meaningful sense. Google's own
//      docs say the installed-app client secret "is obviously not treated as a
//      secret", and RFC 7636 puts it plainly: "secrets provisioned in client
//      binary applications cannot be considered confidential." Anyone can read
//      it out of a distributed binary. What protects the flow is PKCE, which
//      AppAuth performs on every sign-in. See `GoogleOAuthClient.swift`.
//
//      What an extracted client DOES cost you is your project's API quota and
//      the ability for someone to show your app's name on a consent screen. It
//      grants nobody access to anybody's calendar without that person signing
//      in themselves. Lock it down with per-user quotas and an OAuth consent
//      screen that names you - see docs/RELEASING.md.
//
//   6. GOOGLE_AUTH_KEYCHAIN_NAME is any stable string; it names the Keychain
//      item holding the signed-in user's OAuth tokens.
//
//   7. Rebuild (make build). Google Calendar can then be switched on in
//      Preferences > Calendars, alongside macOS Calendar rather than instead of
//      it: both sources can be connected at once and their meetings are merged.
//      A meeting arriving from both is shown once, using the Google copy.
//
// NOTE on scopes: Calendar is a SENSITIVE scope. While your consent screen is in
// "Testing" it works only for accounts listed under "Test users" (capped at
// 100). Publishing to anyone else requires Google's verification review.

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID = 1234567890-abc123example
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET =
GOOGLE_AUTH_KEYCHAIN_NAME = MeetingBarNG.GoogleAuth
